\title{A Bertrand-Postulate Obstruction to Square and \\ Square--Triangular Factorials}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-25}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete and elementary classification of the natural numbers $n$ for which the factorial $n!$ is a perfect square, and for which $n!$ is simultaneously a perfect square and a triangular number. In both cases the answer is the same and maximally restrictive: it happens if and only if $n \le 1$. The proof rests on a single structural fact extracted from \emph{Bertrand's postulate}: for every $n \ge 2$ there is a prime $p$ with $n/2 < p \le n$, and such a prime divides $n!$ to \emph{exact multiplicity one}. Since a perfect square requires every prime to occur to even multiplicity, this lone prime obstructs squareness for all $n \ge 2$. We isolate the obstruction as reusable lemmas, formalize the entire chain in the Lean~4 proof assistant on top of Mathlib, and situate the result against its famous open cousin, the \emph{Brocard--Ramanujan problem} ($n! + 1 = m^2$), whose triangular reformulation we make precise. All results in this paper are fully proved; the Brocard--Ramanujan classification is discussed only as motivation and future work, and is \emph{not} claimed as a theorem here.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Factorials, $n! = \prod_{k=1}^{n} k$, are among the most basic objects in combinatorics, counting the permutations of an $n$-element set. A recurring meta-question in number theory is whether members of a fast-growing integer sequence can also be \emph{figurate} --- perfect squares, triangular numbers, perfect powers, and so on. Such coincidences are rare and rigid, and their classification often reveals a sharp arithmetic obstruction.

This paper answers the question for squares and for the square--triangular intersection. The data is suggestive: the perfect squares begin $1, 4, 9, 16, 25, \dots$, while the factorials are $1, 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, 720, \dots$. The only overlaps are the two trivial $1$'s at $n = 0$ and $n = 1$. We prove this is no accident and admits no large exception.

\begin{theorem}[Square classification, informal]
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $n!$ is a perfect square if and only if $n \le 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Square--triangular classification, informal]
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $n!$ is simultaneously a perfect square and a triangular number if and only if $n \le 1$.
\end{corollary}

The engine is a clean valuation statement: the $p$-adic valuation of $n!$ at a Bertrand prime $p \in (n/2, n]$ is exactly $1$. Because squareness demands even valuations everywhere, a single odd valuation suffices to rule it out. The argument is short, fully constructive in its prime input, and was formally verified in Lean~4.

We deliberately separate what is proved from what is conjectured. The closely related \emph{Brocard--Ramanujan problem} asks for which $n$ is $n! + 1$ a perfect square; only $n \in \{4, 5, 7\}$ are known and the finiteness of this set is a longstanding open problem. We give its exact triangular reformulation (Section~\ref{sec:brocard}) and explain precisely why the ``$+1$'' defeats the valuation method, but we make no claim to resolve it.

\section{Definitions and Preliminaries}

Throughout, $\mathbb{N} = \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}$ and $n! = \prod_{k=1}^n k$ with $0! = 1$.

\begin{definition}[Perfect square]
A natural number $m$ is a \emph{perfect square}, written $\IsSquare(m)$, if there exists $k \in \mathbb{N}$ with $m = k^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Triangular number]
A natural number $m$ is \emph{triangular}, written $\IsTriangular(m)$, if there exists $t \in \mathbb{N}$ with
\[ m = \frac{t(t+1)}{2} = T_t. \]
The triangular numbers are $0, 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, \dots$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$p$-adic valuation]
For a prime $p$ and a positive integer $m$, the \emph{$p$-adic valuation} $\val_p(m)$ is the exponent of $p$ in the prime factorization of $m$; equivalently $\val_p(m) = \max\{e : p^e \mid m\}$. In Lean/Mathlib this is \texttt{Nat.factorization m p}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Legendre's formula]\label{lem:legendre}
For a prime $p$ and $n \ge 1$,
\[ \val_p(n!) = \sum_{i \ge 1} \left\lfloor \frac{n}{p^i} \right\rfloor, \]
a finite sum since $\lfloor n/p^i \rfloor = 0$ once $p^i > n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Bertrand's postulate, half-interval form]\label{thm:bertrand}
For every integer $n \ge 2$ there exists a prime $p$ with
\[ \frac{n}{2} < p \le n, \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad p \le n < 2p. \]
In Mathlib this is available as \texttt{Nat.exists\_prime\_lt\_and\_le\_two\_mul}, applied at $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$.
\end{theorem}

A perfect square has an even valuation at every prime: if $m = k^2$ then $\val_p(m) = 2\,\val_p(k)$ for all $p$. The contrapositive of this observation drives the entire paper.

\subsection{Two worked examples}

It is worth seeing the mechanism concretely before the general proof.

\paragraph{Example A ($n = 10$).} We have $10! = 3{,}628{,}800$. Bertrand's postulate (applied at $\lfloor 10/2 \rfloor = 5$) supplies a prime in $(5, 10]$; take $p = 7$. The only multiple of $7$ among $1, \dots, 10$ is $7$ itself, and the next one, $14$, exceeds $10$. So $\val_7(10!) = \lfloor 10/7 \rfloor + \lfloor 10/49 \rfloor + \cdots = 1$. Since the exponent of $7$ is odd, $10!$ cannot be a perfect square --- indeed $\sqrt{3{,}628{,}800} \approx 1904.94$. (Note $\val_5(10!) = 2 + 0 = 2$ is even, so not \emph{every} prime witnesses the obstruction; the \emph{Bertrand} prime always does.)

\paragraph{Example B ($n = 15$).} Here $\lfloor 15/2 \rfloor = 7$, and Bertrand gives a prime in $(7, 15]$; take $p = 13$. The multiples of $13$ up to $15$ are just $\{13\}$, and $26 > 15$, so $\val_{13}(15!) = 1$. The largest prime $\le n$ is always such a witness, since for $n \ge 2$ it already satisfies $p > n/2$.

\section{The Square Obstruction}

We first record the abstract obstruction, independent of factorials.

\begin{lemma}[Odd-once primes block squares]\label{lem:obstruction}
Let $p$ be prime and $m \in \mathbb{N}$. If $p \mid m$ but $p^2 \nmid m$, then $m$ is not a perfect square.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose for contradiction $m = k^2$. Since $p$ is prime and $p \mid k^2$, Euclid's lemma gives $p \mid k$, hence $p^2 = p \cdot p \mid k \cdot k = k^2 = m$, contradicting $p^2 \nmid m$.
\end{proof}

In Lean this is \texttt{not\_square\_of\_prime\_dvd\_not\_sq\_dvd}, proved by introducing the witness $k$, deriving $p \mid k$ from \texttt{Nat.Prime.dvd\_of\_dvd\_pow}, and concluding $p^2 \mid k^2$ via \texttt{pow\_dvd\_pow\_of\_dvd}.

\section{Exact Multiplicity at a Bertrand Prime}

The crux is that a Bertrand prime occurs in $n!$ exactly once.

\begin{lemma}[Exact multiplicity one]\label{lem:mult-one}
Let $p$ be prime with $p \le n < 2p$. Then
\[ \val_p(n!) = 1, \qquad\text{and in particular}\qquad p^2 \nmid n!. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Legendre's formula (Lemma~\ref{lem:legendre}),
\[ \val_p(n!) = \sum_{i \ge 1} \left\lfloor \frac{n}{p^i} \right\rfloor. \]
We show only the $i = 1$ term survives.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The $i = 1$ term equals $1$.} Since $p \le n$ we have $\lfloor n/p \rfloor \ge 1$. Since $n < 2p$ we have $n/p < 2$, so $\lfloor n/p \rfloor \le 1$. Hence $\lfloor n/p \rfloor = 1$: among $1, 2, \dots, n$ there is exactly one multiple of $p$, namely $p$ itself.
  \item \textbf{All terms $i \ge 2$ vanish.} From $n < 2p \le p \cdot p = p^2$ (using $p \ge 2$), we get $n < p^2 \le p^i$ for $i \ge 2$, so $\lfloor n/p^i \rfloor = 0$.
\end{itemize}
Therefore $\val_p(n!) = 1$. Since $p^2 \mid n!$ would require $\val_p(n!) \ge 2$, we conclude $p^2 \nmid n!$.
\end{proof}

In Lean (\texttt{not\_sq\_dvd\_factorial}) the valuation is computed via \texttt{Nat.factorization\_def} and \texttt{padicValNat\_factorial}, the upper cutoff for the log range comes from \texttt{Nat.log\_lt\_of\_lt\_pow}, and the final non-divisibility is obtained from \texttt{Nat.Prime.pow\_dvd\_iff\_le\_factorization} together with the bound $\lfloor n/p \rfloor < 2$. The combinatorial content is precisely the ``first multiple in, second multiple out'' observation: the next multiple of $p$ after $p$ is $2p > n$.

\section{Main Results}

\begin{theorem}[Factorials are not squares for $n \ge 2$]\label{thm:not-square}
For every $n \ge 2$, $n!$ is not a perfect square.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Bertrand's postulate (Theorem~\ref{thm:bertrand}) applied at $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$, there is a prime $p$ with $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor < p \le 2\lfloor n/2 \rfloor \le n$, hence $p \le n < 2p$. Then $p \mid n!$ because $p \le n$ (so $p$ is one of the factors; \texttt{Nat.dvd\_factorial}), while $p^2 \nmid n!$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:mult-one}. By Lemma~\ref{lem:obstruction}, $n!$ is not a perfect square.
\end{proof}

This is \texttt{factorial\_not\_square\_of\_two\_le} in Lean.

\begin{theorem}[Square classification]\label{thm:square-iff}
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
\[ \IsSquare(n!) \iff n \le 1. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:not-square}: if $n \ge 2$ then $n!$ is not a square; equivalently, a square factorial forces $n \le 1$.
($\Leftarrow$) For $n \in \{0, 1\}$ we have $n! = 1 = 1^2$, so the witness $k = 1$ works.
\end{proof}

This is \texttt{factorial\_square\_iff\_le\_one}.

\begin{theorem}[Square--triangular classification]\label{thm:sq-tri-iff}
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
\[ \big(\IsSquare(n!) \wedge \IsTriangular(n!)\big) \iff n \le 1. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) The conjunction implies $\IsSquare(n!)$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:square-iff} then yields $n \le 1$. (The triangular hypothesis is not needed for this direction --- squareness alone already forces $n \le 1$.)
($\Leftarrow$) For $n \in \{0, 1\}$, $n! = 1$, and $1 = 1^2$ is a square while $1 = \frac{1 \cdot 2}{2} = T_1$ is triangular; so both witnesses ($k = 1$ and $t = 1$) work.
\end{proof}

This is \texttt{factorial\_square\_triangular\_iff\_le\_one}. The corollary is striking precisely because it shows the \emph{square} constraint is the binding one.

\section{The Brocard--Ramanujan Connection}\label{sec:brocard}

The titular motivation is the \emph{Brocard--Ramanujan problem}: determine all $n$ for which
\begin{equation}
  n! + 1 = m^2 \tag{$\star$}
\end{equation}
for some integer $m$. Solutions $(n, m)$ are called \emph{Brown numbers}; the only known ones are
\[ (4, 5):\ 4! + 1 = 25, \qquad (5, 11):\ 5! + 1 = 121, \qquad (7, 71):\ 7! + 1 = 5041. \]
It is conjectured these are the only solutions, verified computationally for $n$ up to roughly $10^{12}$, but the finiteness of the solution set remains \textbf{open}.

\begin{proposition}[Triangular reformulation]\label{prop:tri}
For $n \ge 1$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $n! + 1$ is a perfect square;
  \item $n!/8$ is a triangular number (in particular $8 \mid n!$, which holds for all $n \ge 4$).
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $n!/8 = T_y = y(y+1)/2$ then $n! = 4y(y+1) = (2y+1)^2 - 1$, so $n! + 1 = (2y+1)^2$ is an (odd) square. Conversely, if $n! + 1 = m^2$, then since $n!$ is even for $n \ge 2$, $m$ is odd, say $m = 2y + 1$; expanding, $n! = (2y+1)^2 - 1 = 4y^2 + 4y = 8 \cdot \frac{y(y+1)}{2}$, so $n!/8 = T_y$.
\end{proof}

Thus the three Brown numbers correspond to three triangular values:
\[ \frac{4!}{8} = 3 = T_2, \qquad \frac{5!}{8} = 15 = T_5, \qquad \frac{7!}{8} = 630 = T_{35}. \]
The Brocard--Ramanujan conjecture is exactly the statement that $n \in \{4, 5, 7\}$ are the only $n$ with $n!/8$ triangular.

\paragraph{Why the valuation method does not transfer.} Our proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:square-iff} exploits that we know the prime anatomy of $n!$ exactly; the Bertrand prime $p$ has $\val_p(n!) = 1$, an odd exponent forbidden in a square. But $\gcd(n!, n! + 1) = 1$: adding $1$ destroys all shared prime structure, so $\val_p(n! + 1) = 0$ for that same $p$, and the obstruction evaporates. This is the precise reason the innocuous ``$+1$'' separates a one-paragraph theorem from a 150-year-old open problem.

\subsection{Historical Context}

The study of when factorials meet figurate numbers is old and recurring. Bertrand conjectured his postulate in 1845 after verifying it up to $n = 3{,}000{,}000$; Chebyshev gave the first proof in 1852, and Erd\H{o}s later popularized a short elementary proof using binomial coefficients. Legendre's formula for $\val_p(n!)$ dates to the early nineteenth century and is the standard tool for analyzing the multiplicative structure of factorials. The observation that a prime in the top half of $\{1, \dots, n\}$ appears exactly once in $n!$ is folklore, used for instance to show that the central binomial coefficient $\binom{2n}{n}$ is never a perfect power.

Brocard posed $n! + 1 = m^2$ in 1876 and again in 1885; Ramanujan independently raised it in 1913. The three Brown numbers were known to both, and despite computation to $n$ well beyond $10^{12}$ no fourth solution has appeared. Heuristics on the density of squares near $n!$ suggest the list is complete, but a proof remains elusive. Our contribution gives the \emph{adjacent}, fully resolved statement (about $n!$ rather than $n! + 1$) a clean, machine-checked treatment, and delineates exactly why the two problems differ in difficulty.

\subsection{Computational Verification}

The accompanying \texttt{demo.py} independently checks the theorems on an initial range. For each $n$ it computes $n!$ exactly, tests squareness via an integer square root, tests triangularity via ``$m$ triangular $\iff 8m + 1$ is a square,'' and exhibits the Bertrand prime $p$ with $\val_p(n!) = 1$. Across $0 \le n \le 15$ the only square (and only square-and-triangular) factorials are $n \in \{0, 1\}$, agreeing with Theorems~\ref{thm:square-iff}--\ref{thm:sq-tri-iff}. The script also recovers exactly the Brown numbers $(4, 5), (5, 11), (7, 71)$ and asserts, for every tested $n$, the equivalence of Proposition~\ref{prop:tri}. A companion routine decides squareness of $n!$ in $O(n)$ primality work \emph{without forming the astronomically large integer $n!$}, a direct distillation of the proof: it locates the Bertrand prime and reads off the parity of its (always odd) valuation.

\section{Formalization Notes}

All results of Sections 3--5 are formalized in Lean~4 over Mathlib in the file \texttt{Catalog/NumberTheory/FactorialNotSquare.lean}, namespace \texttt{FactorialNotSquare}. The dependency chain is
\[
\text{Lem.\ref{lem:obstruction}},\ \text{Lem.\ref{lem:mult-one}} \ \Rightarrow\ \text{Thm.\ref{thm:not-square}} \ \Rightarrow\ \text{Thm.\ref{thm:square-iff}} \ \Rightarrow\ \text{Thm.\ref{thm:sq-tri-iff}},
\]
with Lemma~\ref{lem:mult-one} drawing on Bertrand's postulate via \texttt{Nat.exists\_prime\_lt\_and\_le\_two\_mul}. The Lean predicates \texttt{IsSquareNat m := $\exists$ k, m = k\^{}2} and \texttt{IsTriangularNat m := $\exists$ t, m = t*(t+1)/2} match the definitions above. Proposition~\ref{prop:tri} is not formalized in this file (it concerns the open problem and is presented as context).

\section{Applications and Generalizations}

The ``exact multiplicity one'' lemma is a reusable obstruction generator.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Higher powers.} Since $\val_p(n!) = 1$ for the Bertrand prime, and a perfect $k$-th power requires $k \mid \val_q(m)$ for all primes $q$, the same single prime shows $n!$ is never a perfect $k$-th power for any $k \ge 2$ once $n \ge 2$.
  \item \textbf{Figurate numbers.} Many figurate shapes (pentagonal, hexagonal) impose constraints that a lone odd-multiplicity prime can violate; the method extends to ruling these out for large $n$.
  \item \textbf{Structure vs.\ disguise.} Conceptually, $n!$ exposes its factorization by construction and is therefore ``obstructable,'' whereas $n! + 1$ hides its factorization --- the same tension underlying factorization-based cryptography.
\end{itemize}

The unifying principle deserves emphasis. Every ``rigid shape'' a number can take --- square, cube, $k$-th power, and many figurate forms --- imposes a constraint on the \emph{exponent vector} $(\val_2(m), \val_3(m), \val_5(m), \dots)$ of $m$. Squares demand all entries even; cubes, all divisible by three; perfect $k$-th powers, all divisible by $k$. Bertrand's postulate hands us a coordinate of this vector that we can pin down exactly for $m = n!$: the Bertrand prime's entry is precisely $1$. A single coordinate equal to $1$ violates \emph{every} divisibility-by-$k$ constraint with $k \ge 2$ at once. Thus one lemma --- ``exact multiplicity one'' --- refutes squareness, cubeness, and all higher-power-ness of $n!$ in one stroke, and with light congruence bookkeeping attacks figurate shapes too. This is why we regard Lemma~\ref{lem:mult-one}, rather than any single classification theorem, as the reusable core.

\section{Discussion and Future Work}

The proven theorems are tight and unconditional. The natural frontier is the Brocard--Ramanujan problem and its relatives, which the valuation method alone cannot reach. Four concrete directions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Bounded computational certificates for Brown numbers}: checked, bounded statements (``no Brown numbers for $8 \le n \le N$'') as reusable parameterized certificates with valuation-based pruning.
  \item \textbf{Pell recurrences for square--triangular numbers}: formalize the classical theory ($0, 1, 36, 1225, \dots$) via $x^2 - 2y^2 = 1$ and $a_{k+1} = 34a_k - a_{k-1} + 2$.
  \item \textbf{Cataloging factorial figurate obstructions via prime intervals}: a library showing $n!$ is never a perfect $k$-th power and never various figurate numbers for large $n$.
  \item \textbf{Reusable Mathlib lemmas}: a polished general lemma that a prime $p$ with $p \le n < 2p$ has $\val_p(n!) = 1$, plus corollaries.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

We classified, completely and elementarily, the square and square--triangular factorials: both occur exactly when $n \le 1$. The proof distills to one idea --- a Bertrand prime in $(n/2, n]$ divides $n!$ precisely once, and one odd exponent forecloses squareness. The same idea fails by a hair's breadth for $n! + 1$, the Brocard--Ramanujan problem, illuminating exactly where elementary methods end and a famous mystery begins.

